Pinguecula
Opthalmology & ENT
Yellowish conjunctival nodule, No corneal involvement, Nasal perilimbal location

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-036
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, painful red eye localized to a visible lump, causing intense anxiety regarding impending blindness or ocular tumor.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with an acutely inflamed, painful, localized nodular lesion on the surface of the eye, generating extreme distress. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to definitively differentiate a benign, inflamed pinguecula (pingueculitis) from sight-threatening nodular scleritis, peripheral ulcerative keratitis, or an acutely infected retained foreign body, and to provide immediate targeted therapy to suppress the intense, acute inflammation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "There is a yellow lump on the white of my eye that suddenly turned bright red and feels like burning gravel every time I blink."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a long-standing, previously asymptomatic, yellowish, slightly raised soft nodule on the bulbar conjunctiva, strictly located on the nasal side. Tonight, the lesion became acutely swollen, intensely erythematous, and painful (pingueculitis). Endorses a severe, constant foreign body sensation and localized dryness. Strong occupational history of prolonged, cumulative exposure to ultraviolet radiation and wind (outdoor construction worker). Denies any visual impairment, deep aching orbital pain, or purulent discharge.
Functional Status: Highly distracted by the constant gritty pain and cosmetic appearance; unable to sleep.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety regarding an ocular malignancy or permanent damage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 85 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Anxious, frequently blinking and gently rubbing the medial aspect of the affected eye.
Physical Exam: Visual acuity is 6/6 bilaterally. Slit-lamp equivalent exam with ophthalmoscope reveals a distinct, yellowish, raised soft nodule on the nasal bulbar conjunctiva adjacent to the limbus. Absolutely no crossover corneal involvement or neovascularization onto the cornea. Pronounced, focal conjunctival injection specifically surrounds the nodular lesion. The surrounding sclera is stark white, with no deep episcleral or scleral engorgement. Cornea is crystal clear. Pupils equal and reactive.
Environmental Observations: Patient works outdoors; safety glasses visibly absent from personal protective equipment bag on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pingueculitis (Acutely Inflamed Pinguecula) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Nodular Scleritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of severe, deep, boring eye pain that awakens the patient from sleep, and the inflammation is entirely superficial (blanching vessels) rather than deep, immobile scleral vessels.

Pterygium - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The lesion being strictly confined to the conjunctiva adjacent to the limbus without any fibrovascular tissue crossing over Bowman's layer onto the clear cornea.

Retained Conjunctival Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a discrete, fleshy, yellowish subepithelial nodule rather than a hard, particulate matter embedded in the mucosa.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, highly localized, painful ocular inflammatory episode mimicking a severe sight-threatening condition or malignancy to the layperson. Urgent direct clinical evaluation was required to definitively rule out nodular scleritis or corneal ulceration. The physical examination confirmed the pathology was entirely superficial and restricted to a classic nasal pinguecula, experiencing an acute inflammatory flare (pingueculitis) due to environmental desiccation. By diagnosing this benign condition, the urgent visit prevented an emergency department presentation, immediately relieved the severe anxiety of suspected malignancy, and provided safe, localized anti-inflammatory treatment for the acute symptomatic distress.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed intensive preservative-free lubricating eye drops to be used hourly. Prescribed a short, 5-day course of topical NSAID drops (Ketorolac 0.5%) QID to aggressively reduce the acute focal inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically protect the disrupted tear film over the raised nodule, the patient must apply a thick, paraffin-based lubricating eye ointment directly to the lower eyelid margin every night exactly 5 minutes before sleep. The immediate, soothing relief from the friction of the eyelid over the inflamed lump provides positive reinforcement, ensuring compliance with the heavy lubrication necessary to heal the localized dry spot (dellen) and calm the flare.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP or Optometrist review in 7 days to ensure the acute inflammation has resolved.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the redness spreads to involve the entire white of the eye, if vision suddenly blurs, if severe deep aching pain begins, or if a white spot appears on the colored part of the eye.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a benign, localized inflammation. If the lump grows onto the clear part of the eye, it becomes a pterygium requiring surgical review.
Clinical Course: The severe redness and gritty pain will subside significantly over 3-5 days with the anti-inflammatory drops and heavy lubrication. The underlying yellow lump will remain permanently.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If your vision changes, or the pain turns from a scratchy feeling into a deep, severe ache, you must seek immediate medical attention.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Years of sun and wind exposure have caused a small, benign callus to form on the white of your eye. Because it is raised, your eyelid rubs against it, and it has dried out and become acutely inflamed. The drops will reduce the swelling and lubricate the surface.
Actionable Steps: Wear wrap-around UV-protective sunglasses at all times when outdoors. Use the lubricating ointment every night before bed. Stop using any over-the-counter "redness relief" eye drops immediately.

SMS INFORMATION

